So you are saying that the only goals of Nethack that you find worth
rewarding with score points are those that can be generated by
farming? No wonder you dislike farming so much! So extinctionists
rock, but lowest kills, didn't kill uniques, ascended with 80 maxHP,
all that stuff is "alternate victory conditions" that you don't take
seriously, right?

> >This, then, must be what you consider an acceptable level of farming.
>
> Yes.  You may notice that the approach to "farming" now looks remarkably
> similar to "extinctionist" -- where you have to spend far more actual
> real-time effort to obtain similar results, and you can't simply paste a
> string over and over into the buffer over a day or two.

The scriptability is pretty much the only part of pudding farming as
it now exists that I find objectionable. As Topi said, "farming should
be boring". Perhaps a 1/100 chance that hitting a pudding will cause
you to teleport randomly, or exchange places with the pudding, or
induce stunning or confusion (with the associated chance of random
movement), or anything that would be manageable in "by-hand" play but
would break up the flow of copy-pasted commands?

> > Or you could say that by programming
> > in a "bypass puzzle" button, the designers have given those of us who
> > can't deal with timed-puzzle-solving an option that allows us to enjoy
> > their game (and spend money on it).
>
> Well, realistically, they programmed in a "bypass puzzle" option so they
> could sell strategy guides at $19.95 and the lazy kids out there who
> can't be bothered to try to figure a puzzle out from the word 'go' would
> get stuck and have Mom buy the strategy guide.

Actually I found this little tidbit free and gratis on the
manufacturer's very own webpage (although well buried). I think that
there are a fair number of players that don't enjoy timed puzzles, and
presumably game designers know this.

> > Nethack is a game. It's not a sport or a competition or a standardized
> > test. "Shortcuts" are fine if you want to use them.
>
> However, it _is_ a competition at least twice a year; the devnull
> tournament has been around for a long, long time, and the June NAO
> tournament certainly draws a _ton_ of players compared to normal server
> operation.
>
> There is enough interest in formalized competition such that taking
> competition into consideration in the design is a good thing.

So fine. Let tournament play use a special version; from what I've
seen on RGRN it is not unusual to have special elements in tournaments
anyway, right? But for the rest of the time, when it *isn't* a
competition, why should standardization apply?

Look: you go set up Sporkhack just the way you like it. I'll keep
playing Nethack. You'll be happy, I'll be happy. If and or when the
DevTeam nerfs pudding farming, I'm sure someone will write a patch to
reintroduce it. So we can all be happy, farmers and nonfarmers alike.
Just don't tell me what I should be playing :)

